## Scripts

Run write-mode formatting:

```bash
./scripts/format.sh
```

Formatting and generation must converge: if a formatter and generator keep changing the same generated file back and forth, exclude that generated path from formatter globs, post-format during generation, or make the generator emit formatter-stable output.

Run local changed-scope validation:

```bash
./scripts/validate-changed-scope.mjs
```

Before marking implementation work complete during local development, `validate-changed-scope.mjs` is mandatory. It selects checks from changed files, including frontend format/lint, related tests where possible, impacted Playwright E2E subsets, backend checks, and docs/governance checks.

Run full finalization:

```bash
./scripts/finalize.sh
```

`finalize.sh` is check-only and should not modify files. Use it for CI, pre-merge full-gate validation, or an explicit request for full finalization.

{% if include_layering_validator -%}
Architecture layering is enforced by `scripts/validate-layering.mjs` as part of changed-scope validation when related files changed and as part of full finalization. Update `docs/specs/architecture-layering-v0.1.md` before changing layer rules.

{% endif -%}
{% if include_api_contract | default(false) -%}
Regenerate backend OpenAPI and frontend API types:

```bash
./scripts/generate-api-contract.sh
```

Changed-scope validation and full finalization check committed generated contracts when API or schema files are affected. Do not hand-write frontend DTOs for backend API responses.

{% endif -%}
Before changing project conventions, validation scripts, architecture rules, generated schemas, or agent-facing docs, read `docs/specs/agent-governance-v0.1.md`.

{% if frontend_stack != 'none' -%}
For frontend projects, explicitly evaluate Playwright E2E during scaffold planning. If it is relevant now, install and wire it using `docs/tasks/e2e-testing.md`; if not, record why it is deferred and what will make it active.

{% if include_playwright_e2e | default(false) -%}
Run E2E tests:

```bash
pnpm --dir {{ frontend_dir }} test:e2e
```

`finalize.sh` includes the full E2E gate. Local changed-scope validation may run impacted E2E subsets selected by `@impact-*` tags, `scripts/playwright-impact-rules.mjs`, and `scripts/select-playwright-impact-tests.mjs`. Keep `{{ playwright_config_path }}`, the impact rules, the impact-tag validator, and the frontend E2E scripts aligned.

{% endif -%}
{% endif -%}
For documentation, contract, and code alignment checks, use `docs/tasks/alignment-check.md`.

Run the local stack:

```bash
./scripts/dev.sh
```

Do not run `./scripts/dev.sh` or frontend `dev` commands just because frontend files changed or to leave a preview running for the user. Start a live server only when the user asks to run the app or when runtime inspection, debugging, or browser-based verification needs it.

When tests or runtime checks require backend or frontend services, do not assume the user's own server is already running or safe to reuse. Start the needed services through a dedicated script or process using an available isolated port, and shut down only the processes you started.

Check a running local stack:

```bash
./scripts/check-health.sh
```

Run a temporary startup smoke check:

```bash
./scripts/smoke.sh
```

`smoke.sh` starts the configured local services, checks configured endpoints, and kills only the PIDs it started. Run it after changes to startup, routing, environment wiring, or cross-app integration.

Governance smoke tests must use minimal fixtures. Do not copy the full repository into a temp directory, and do not copy build outputs, `node_modules`, Rust `target`, Playwright artifacts, or other generated directories unless the test specifically needs them.

## Credential and Tooling Safety

Do not change global Git, GitHub, or credential-manager configuration by default. In particular,
do not run commands such as `gh auth setup-git`, change `credential.helper`, overwrite Git remotes,
or alter global auth settings unless the user has explicitly approved that exact change after being
told how it can affect existing VS Code, Keychain, GitHub CLI, or repository-specific workflows.

## Interaction Design Discussions

For frontend, HCI, product-flow, or interaction-design discussions:

- First verify the current product flow in local code, specs, or status notes
  before proposing changes. Do not rely on memory or a generic mental model of
  the app.
- Explain the design naturally as usual, then add a concise natural-language
  diff against the current design so the proposal is easy to review.
- Use diff-style formatting only for real changes; mark unchanged areas as "no
  change" in prose instead.
- Keep interaction design separate from implementation details until the
  interaction model is clear. Discuss code structure only after the current flow
  and proposed UX diff are understood.

